# III. The primitive concept: constrained possibility

Let a physical system (A) have state space:

[
X_A
]

and transition kernel:

[
P(x_{t+1}\mid x_t,e_t)
]

where (e_t) denotes environmental conditions.

A constraint (C) changes the distribution of accessible futures:

[
P_C(x_{t+1}\mid x_t,e_t)
\neq
P_0(x_{t+1}\mid x_t,e_t)
]

where (P_0) is the transition distribution without that constraint.

Define constraint strength:

[
\mathcal C_A
============

D_{\mathrm{KL}}
\left(
P_C(X_{t+1}\mid x_t)
\parallel
P_0(X_{t+1}\mid x_t)
\right)
]

A physical structure is informationally effective when it preserves a difference that changes future probability distributions.

Thus:

[
\boxed{
I_{\mathrm{eff}}
================

\text{a persistent difference that makes a counterfactual difference}
}
]

Examples include:

* a catalyst changing reaction pathways;
* a membrane changing transport probabilities;
* a gene-regulatory state changing expression;
* a synaptic configuration changing future activation;
* an institutional record changing later decisions.

Information is not a second substance inserted into matter.

It is:

[
\boxed{
\text{the transition-constraining organization of matter and energy}
}
]

