# V. Irreversibility and the arrow of information

Doyle is right that temporality is essential.

A logically reversible mapping:

[
x_{t+1}=f(x_t)
]

with invertible (f) preserves complete state information:

[
x_t=f^{-1}(x_{t+1})
]

But real memory formation, measurement, error correction, metabolism and learning involve dissipative physical processes.

Let entropy production be:

[
\Sigma
======

\Delta S_{\mathrm{system}}
+
\Delta S_{\mathrm{environment}}
\geq0
]

A stable memory requires physical separation between distinguishable states and resistance to spontaneous erasure. Establishing and maintaining this distinction generally incurs energetic and entropic costs.

However, the following inference is invalid:

[
\Sigma>0
\Rightarrow
\text{meaningful information creation}
]

Friction and uncontrolled decay also produce entropy.

The correct relation is:

[
\boxed{
\text{Irreversibility is generally necessary for durable historical records,
but it is not sufficient for adaptive information.}
}
]

A historical record exists when:

[
I(M_t;X_{t-\tau})>0
]

A useful record additionally requires:

[
I(M_t;G_A)>0
]

or:

[
I(M_t;Y_{t+\Delta})>0
]

for system-relevant future variables.

