# VI. The generalized two-stage architecture

Doyle’s Cogito model can be scientifically reconstructed as an exploration–selection cycle.

Let (M_t) be the system’s current model or memory.

## Stage 1: Alternative generation

[
Z_t
\sim
q_\theta(z\mid M_t,E_t)
]

The generator may produce:

* actions;
* hypotheses;
* motor plans;
* interpretations;
* morphological trajectories;
* symbolic constructions.

Variation need not be purely random.

Decompose it into:

[
Z_t
===

Z_t^{\mathrm{directed}}
+
Z_t^{\mathrm{stochastic}}
+
Z_t^{\mathrm{recombinational}}
]

Modern exploration research distinguishes random exploration from directed exploration aimed at reducing uncertainty. They are behaviourally and computationally separable rather than one undifferentiated source of chance. ([PubMed][7])

## Stage 2: Evaluation and selection

An evaluator assigns:

[
Q(z\mid M_t,G_t,C_t)
]

where:

* (G_t): goals or preferred states;
* (C_t): practical constraints;
* (M_t): learned model.

Selection may be:

[
z_t^*
=====

\arg\max_z Q(z\mid M_t,G_t,C_t)
]

or probabilistic:

[
P(z_t=z)
========

\frac{e^{\beta Q(z)}}{\sum_{z'}e^{\beta Q(z')}}
]

## Stage 3: Enactment

The selected option changes the world and system:

[
x_{t+1}
=======

\Phi(x_t,z_t^*,e_t)
]

## Stage 4: Learning

Prediction or outcome error updates memory:

[
\delta_t
========

r_t+\gamma V(x_{t+1})-V(x_t)
]

[
M_{t+1}
=======

U(M_t,\delta_t,x_t,z_t^*)
]

The complete architecture is therefore:

[
\boxed{
\text{generate}
\rightarrow
\text{evaluate}
\rightarrow
\text{enact}
\rightarrow
\text{learn}
}
]

Doyle’s original two stages become four because selection without feedback does not explain cumulative agency.

