# VII. Randomness is neither freedom nor irrelevant noise

Doyle argues that indeterminacy creates alternative possibilities while an adequately determined will chooses among them. This correctly avoids placing randomness directly at the final moment of action. ([Information Philosopher][2])

But quantum randomness is not known to be required.

Alternative generation can arise from:

* deterministic chaos;
* computational underdetermination;
* partial observability;
* neural noise;
* stochastic sampling;
* learned generative models;
* combinatorial recombination.

Let generator entropy be:

[
H(Z\mid M,E)
]

If:

[
H(Z\mid M,E)=0
]

the system generates only one candidate.

But merely increasing entropy does not improve agency:

[
H(Z\mid M,E)\uparrow
\not\Rightarrow
\operatorname{Agency}\uparrow
]

Too little variation produces rigidity.

Too much uncontrolled variation destroys coherence.

Agency requires bounded generativity:

[
0
<
H(Z\mid M,E)
<
H_{\max}
]

combined with effective selection:

[
I(Z^*;G\mid M,E)>0
]

Thus define creative control:

[
\mathcal K
==========

H(Z\mid M,E)
\times
I(Z^*;G\mid M,E)
\times
R_{\mathrm{learning}}
]

where (R_{\mathrm{learning}}) measures whether outcomes improve future generation and evaluation.

A random generator without competent selection has:

[
H(Z)\gg0
]

but:

[
I(Z^*;G)\approx0
]

A rigid controller has:

[
I(Z^*;G)>0
]

in familiar conditions but:

[
H(Z)\approx0
]

and cannot adapt.

The highest agency lies in a viable region balancing exploration and control.

