# IX. Free will reconstructed

Doyle defines free will through open alternatives plus adequately determined selection. The philosophical literature likewise distinguishes alternative possibilities from self-determination, although their necessity and interpretation remain disputed. ([Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy][8])

CSID does not claim metaphysical freedom from all causation.

It defines **operational freedom** as expanded endogenous counterfactual control.

Let:

[
\Pi_A(x)
]

be the policies available to agent (A) in state (x).

Define option breadth:

[
B_A(x)=\log|\Pi_A(x)|
]

Define policy efficacy:

[
E_A
===

I(do(\Pi_A);X_{t+\tau})
]

Define reason sensitivity:

[
R_A
===

I(\Pi_A;R\mid X)
]

where (R) represents reasons, evidence and goals.

Define self-modification:

[
S_A
===

I(M_{t+1};\operatorname{Evaluation}_t)
]

Then:

[
\boxed{
F_A
===

B_A
\cdot
E_A
\cdot
R_A
\cdot
S_A
}
]

Operational freedom increases when the agent:

* can generate more viable alternatives;
* can produce different futures through selection;
* selects in response to reasons;
* learns from the consequences;
* can revise its own policies and goals.

Randomness can increase (B_A) but does not guarantee (E_A), (R_A) or (S_A).

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
\text{Freedom is controlled counterfactual reach,
not mere unpredictability.}
}
]

This is compatible with causal determination at many implementation levels. It is also compatible with stochasticity. It does not resolve every question about ultimate moral responsibility.

