# X. Life as recursive constraint construction

Doyle defines living systems as active information structures that use information to manage flows of matter and energy and preserve themselves. He associates biological purpose with teleonomy rather than an externally imposed cosmic telos. ([Information Philosopher][9])

CSID sharpens this.

A dissipative structure can persist because environmental flows sustain it:

[
E\rightarrow A
]

A living organization additionally contains constraints that contribute to rebuilding the constraints that maintain it:

[
\mathcal C_A
\rightsquigarrow
\mathcal C_A
]

Define recursive constraint closure:

[
\forall c_i\in\mathcal C_A,
\quad
\exists S_i\subseteq\mathcal C_A
:
S_i\rightsquigarrow c_i
]

A living system also preserves inherited information:

[
M_{t+1}
=======

\operatorname{CopyModify}(M_t)
]

and uses present information to regulate its future:

[
a_t
===

\pi(x_t,M_t,G_t)
]

Therefore:

[
\boxed{
\text{Life is not simply low entropy.
It is a recursively maintained system of historically selected constraints.}
}
]

This distinguishes an organism from a crystal.

A crystal possesses ordered structure.

An organism:

* senses deviations;
* performs work to correct them;
* reconstructs components;
* preserves inherited constraints;
* changes its future policies through evolution or learning.

