# XII. The Experience Recorder and Reproducer, corrected

Doyle’s ERR model contains an important rejection of the crude brain-as-digital-computer metaphor. He emphasizes distributed recording and partial reactivation of embodied experience rather than a central observer inspecting stored representations. ([Information Philosopher][10])

But literal recording and playback are too simple.

Memory is generally:

* selective;
* compressed;
* distributed;
* reconstructive;
* context-dependent;
* modified during retrieval.

Replace ERR with:

[
\textbf{Experience-Conditioned Generative Reconstructor}
]

or **ECGR**.

Let an experience generate neural and bodily update:

[
M_{t+1}
=======

U(M_t,O_t,A_t,V_t)
]

where:

* (O_t): observation;
* (A_t): action;
* (V_t): significance or valence.

Later recall is not retrieval of an unchanged record:

[
O_t\neq\operatorname{Read}(M)
]

It is reconstruction:

[
\hat O_t
\sim
P(O\mid M_t,C_t,Q_t)
]

where:

* (C_t): current context;
* (Q_t): present query or task.

Imagination uses the same generative machinery with weaker sensory constraint:

[
\hat O_{\mathrm{imagined}}
\sim
P(O\mid M,G)
]

Deliberation uses it to generate future trajectories:

[
\hat X_{t+\tau}^{(\pi)}
\sim
P(X_{t+\tau}\mid X_t,M_t,\pi)
]

Thus Doyle’s recorder–reproducer architecture becomes:

[
\boxed{
\text{experience}
\rightarrow
\text{model update}
\rightarrow
\text{contextual reconstruction}
\rightarrow
\text{counterfactual generation}
}
]

