# XIII. Knowledge as error-correcting information

Doyle distinguishes information from its physical embodiment and treats knowledge as preserved, communicable information. ([Information Philosopher][11])

The phrase “information is neither matter nor energy” is acceptable only if it means that one abstract pattern can have multiple physical realizations.

For example:

[
p
]

may be embodied as:

[
{
\text{ink},
\text{voltage},
\text{magnetization},
\text{neural activity}
}
]

But every causally active token must be physically realized.

Knowledge requires more than stored information.

Let (M) be a model and (E) evidence.

Define predictive adequacy:

[
\mathcal P(M)
=============

-\mathbb E[\mathcal L(M,E_{\mathrm{future}})]
]

Define intervention adequacy:

[
\mathcal I(M)
=============

P(G\mid do(a_M))
]

Define calibration:

[
\mathcal C(M)
=============

1-
|P(\text{correct}\mid c)-c|
]

for confidence (c).

Define error-correction capacity:

[
\mathcal E(M)
=============

P(M\rightarrow M'\mid\operatorname{counterevidence})
]

Then:

[
\boxed{
\operatorname{Knowledge}(M)
\propto
\mathcal P(M)
\mathcal I(M)
\mathcal C(M)
\mathcal E(M)
}
]

Knowledge is not simply information accumulation.

A database of falsehoods contains information.

Knowledge is information organized into models that remain reliable under prediction, intervention and correction.

