# XV. Causal emergence without mystical information

Doyle often says that information controls matter and energy.

Taken literally, this risks treating information as an additional causal entity.

The better formulation is:

[
\boxed{
\text{A higher-level informational description identifies organized constraints
implemented by lower-level physical processes.}
}
]

Let microstate be (X) and macrostate:

[
M=f(X)
]

A macro-description is scientifically useful when it has greater predictive or causal informativeness for the chosen interventions:

[
I(do(M_t);M_{t+1})

>

I(do(X_t);X_{t+1})
]

after suitable normalization or coarse-graining.

Research on causal emergence and information decomposition shows that macro-level variables may capture causal organization or synergistic information not transparently represented by element-wise descriptions, although the result depends on the measure and intervention framework. ([Royal Society Publishing][12])

This does not imply that an abstract pattern floats above physics and pushes particles.

It means:

[
\boxed{
\text{The physically implemented organization has causal powers most efficiently
expressed at a higher descriptive scale.}
}
]

---

# XVI. Mental causation

Doyle rejects standard mind–brain identity theory and describes mind as an immaterial informational process embodied in the material brain. He then assigns downward causal efficacy to this informational level. ([Information Philosopher][13])

CSID removes the dualistic residue.

Let a decision process be one event (d).

It has:

[
\operatorname{Micro}(d)
=======================

\text{neural, bodily and molecular realization}
]

[
\operatorname{Macro}(d)
=======================

\text{reason-sensitive choice among modelled alternatives}
]

These are not two causes:

[
d_{\mathrm{mental}}
+
d_{\mathrm{physical}}
\rightarrow a
]

They are two descriptions of one causally organized process:

[
\boxed{
d_{\mathrm{mental}}
\equiv_{\mathrm{token}}
d_{\mathrm{physical}}
}
]

The reason causes the action when the neural–bodily process implements a counterfactual dependence on that reason:

[
P(a\mid do(r))
\neq
P(a\mid do(\neg r))
]

The causal work is performed by the embodied organization.

The reason is not causally excluded merely because it is a macro-level description.

---

# XVII. Consciousness: the limit of Information Philosophy

Doyle often describes consciousness in terms of awareness, attention, experience recording and information-guided response. ([Information Philosopher][14])

This can explain:

* access;
* discrimination;
* memory;
* attention;
* report;
* action selection;
* counterfactual modelling.

It does not explain why these operations are phenomenally present.

Therefore:

[
\operatorname{InformationProcessing}(A)
\not\Rightarrow
\operatorname{Phenomenal}(A)
]

without an additional psychophysical principle.

CSID defines a candidate conscious process by:

[
\Chi(A)
=======

f(
\text{integration},
\text{temporal depth},
\text{global availability},
\text{self-modelling},
\text{valuation},
\text{counterfactual control}
)
]

But:

[
\Chi(A)>c
]

is only a bearer criterion unless independently connected to phenomenality.

The scientifically honest statement is:

[
\boxed{
\text{Information dynamics may identify the organization of conscious access
and agency, but it does not derive phenomenal existence.}
}
]

