# XVIII. Value: preservation is not yet goodness

Doyle proposes a cosmic value:

[
\text{Good}
\approx
\text{information creation and preservation}
]

and:

[
\text{Evil}
\approx
\text{destruction of organized information}
]

This fails as a general moral theory.

A virus, tumour, oppressive institution or elaborate deception may contain and preserve substantial organization.

Therefore:

[
I(x)\uparrow
\not\Rightarrow
V(x)\uparrow
]

Destruction can also be beneficial:

* eliminating a pathogen;
* correcting a false belief;
* dismantling a harmful institution;
* apoptosis during development;
* forgetting maladaptive information.

Thus:

[
\operatorname{Preservation}(x)
\not\Rightarrow
\operatorname{Good}(x)
]

## Corrected value theory

Objective biological value begins when outcomes matter for a system’s continued organization.

For organism (A), define viability set:

[
\mathcal V_A
============

{x:A\text{ can continue to exist and function from }x}
]

An outcome has functional value:

[
v_A(o)
======

\log
\frac{
P(X_{t+\tau}\in\mathcal V_A\mid o)
}{
P(X_{t+\tau}\in\mathcal V_A)
}
]

This yields system-relative biological value.

Moral value requires additional constraints concerning multiple subjects:

[
V_{\mathrm{moral}}
==================

F(
\text{welfare},
\text{agency},
\text{fairness},
\text{truth},
\text{relationships},
\text{ecological viability}
)
]

Information preservation matters instrumentally because it can sustain:

* agents;
* knowledge;
* capacities;
* cultural memory;
* ecosystems.

But the moral object is not information quantity itself.

The corrected principle is:

[
\boxed{
\text{Preserve and enlarge the conditions under which sentient and autonomous
systems can develop, learn, cooperate and correct error.}
}
]

