# XIX. The law of adaptive information accumulation

We can now state the core theory.

Let (A) be an open system with:

* generator (G);
* selector (S);
* memory (M);
* controller (\Pi);
* constraints (C).

At time (t):

[
z_t\sim G(M_t,E_t)
]

[
z_t^*\sim S(z_t\mid C_t,M_t)
]

[
x_{t+1}=\Phi(x_t,z_t^*,E_t)
]

[
M_{t+1}=U(M_t,x_t,z_t^*,x_{t+1})
]

Define future-control information:

[
J_t
===

I(M_t;G_{t:t+\tau}\mid E_t)
]

where (G_{t:t+\tau}) denotes future goal attainment or viability.

## Adaptive information accumulation

[
\boxed{
\Delta J_t
==========

J_{t+1}-J_t
}
]

A system accumulates adaptive information when:

[
\mathbb E[\Delta J_t]>0
]

over an appropriate interval.

## Necessary architecture

Sustained adaptive accumulation requires:

[
\boxed{
\operatorname{Variation}
\land
\operatorname{Selection}
\land
\operatorname{Memory}
\land
\operatorname{Feedback}
}
]

## Open-endedness

An adaptive process becomes open-ended when it can modify not only its policies but its generator and evaluator:

[
G_{t+1}
=======

U_G(G_t,\text{experience})
]

[
S_{t+1}
=======

U_S(S_t,\text{experience})
]

and potentially its goals:

[
C_{t+1}
=======

U_C(C_t,\text{reflection})
]

This is the transition from adaptation to reflexive agency.

