# XX. The central theorem

## Constraint-selected novelty theorem

Assume a system (A) possesses:

1. a set of possible variations (Z);
2. a selection function sensitive to constraint (C);
3. physically persistent memory (M);
4. feedback from selected outcomes to future generation;
5. multiple possible future trajectories.

Then (A) can accumulate information about how to remain within or reach (C)-compatible states.

### Proof

Variation produces:

[
q_t(z)
]

Selection conditions persistence on (C):

[
q_t(z\mid C)
\neq
q_t(z)
]

Therefore selected outcomes contain information about (C):

[
I(Z_{\mathrm{selected}};C)>0
]

Memory stores part of this dependence:

[
I(M_{t+1};Z_{\mathrm{selected}})>0
]

Feedback makes future generation conditional on memory:

[
q_{t+1}(z\mid M_{t+1})
\neq
q_t(z)
]

If the stored dependence generalizes, then:

[
P(C\mid M_{t+1})

>

P(C\mid M_t)
]

Therefore the system’s history increasingly constrains future variation toward (C)-compatible outcomes:

[
\Delta J_t>0
]

Hence adaptive information accumulates.

QED.

This is a formal generalization of Doyle’s two-stage architecture.

