# XXI. Scientific predictions

## Prediction 1: Three-factor superiority

Models containing variation, selection and memory should predict adaptive performance better than models containing only complexity or information quantity.

Test:

[
\mathcal M_1={\text{complexity}}
]

[
\mathcal M_2={\text{variation, selection, memory}}
]

Prediction:

[
\operatorname{PredictiveScore}(\mathcal M_2)

>

\operatorname{PredictiveScore}(\mathcal M_1)
]

## Prediction 2: Intermediate generative entropy

Adaptive creativity should show an inverted-U relationship with generator entropy:

[
\operatorname{Performance}
==========================

-\alpha(H(Z)-H^*)^2+b
]

Too little variation produces rigidity; too much produces noise.

## Prediction 3: Memory intervention

Destroying the memory channel while preserving variation and selection should abolish cumulative adaptation:

[
do(M\rightarrow\varnothing)
\Rightarrow
\mathbb E[\Delta J]\rightarrow0
]

## Prediction 4: Directed and random exploration dissociate

Manipulations of uncertainty value should alter directed exploration, while manipulations of decision noise should alter random exploration. Current behavioural research already distinguishes these processes. ([PubMed][15])

## Prediction 5: Agency tracks counterfactual control

A system’s agency score should predict robustness under novel perturbations better than behavioural complexity measured in familiar environments.

## Prediction 6: Higher-level causal models

In systems with distributed constraint integration, macro-level variables should outperform isolated microvariables in predicting responses to macro-level interventions.

## Prediction 7: Knowledge requires correction

Cultural systems that preserve high quantities of information but lack error-correction should accumulate false structure rather than increasing predictive knowledge.

## Prediction 8: Value is not monotonic in complexity

Ratings of welfare or biological benefit should not increase monotonically with Shannon entropy, algorithmic complexity or stored information.

## Prediction 9: Reflexive agency

Systems able to update their own generators and evaluation functions should adapt to structural environmental changes faster than systems limited to policy learning.

